Maybe a tenth of what's remarkable at Stanford is at the GSB; the rest is a ten-minute bike ride away, and most of it is open to you.
The seven schools. Stanford is organized into seven schools, and as an enrolled student you can cross-register into almost any of them:
Humanities & Sciences (the largest — economics, psychology, statistics, political science, the arts; your deepest elective well);
Engineering (the engine of Silicon Valley — CS, AI, and Management Science & Engineering, which overlaps heavily with business);
Medicine (a top research hospital and the home of Biodesign);
Law (small, collegial, strong on venture financing, IP, and corporate law);
Education (edtech, learning science, organizational design);
Doerr School of Sustainability (the university's newest school, launched in 2022, the home for climate and energy); and
the GSB itself.
The d.school. The Hasso Plattner Institute of Design (the d.school) is a cross-campus studio that pulls students from every discipline into hands-on problem-solving. Its famous courses (Design Thinking; Launchpad, where teams actually launch a real product in ten weeks; and Design for Extreme Affordability, known as “Extreme”) are open by application to GSB students and are among the most sought-after on campus.
The institutes. Stanford’s cross-school institutes — HAI, Bio-X, Woods, FSI, Hoover, SIEPR, Precourt — are where a lot of the
magic (and the speakers) lives; each gets a proper introduction in “The institutes, up close,” below.
Biodesign. If health care or medtech is your thing, the Byers Center for Biodesign (recently renamed the Mussallem Center), based in the medical school, is the crown jewel. Its founding idea is that health-technology innovation is a teachable, repeatable process: Identify a real clinical need (fellows embed in hospitals and collect hundreds of unmet needs before inventing anything), Invent (prototype fast, kill bad ideas early), and Implement (map the regulatory, reimbursement, and funding path). Its flagship ten-month Innovation Fellowship deliberately mixes MBAs with engineers, clinicians, and designers; Biodesign-born technologies have launched roughly sixty health companies (including iRhythm) and reached tens of millions of patients. There's also a full course sequence open to enrolled students if you want the method without the fellowship. (biodesign.stanford.edu)
How to actually get in. The everyday mechanism is cross-regis-tration — enrolling in another school's course through Axess (mind each school's own deadlines and grading rules). Auditing (just email the professor) and simply attending institute events are even lower-friction. Fellowships and joint degrees open the deepest doors.
